  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_c35dbcb10f7f4e47af8454ad5949cde8",
      "statement": "The status vocabulary is six values of which four are distinct absences — not_found, not_searchable, out_of_scope and found_only_in_superseded — each carrying a different instruction to the caller, because collapsing them into an empty list makes the agent guess.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/core/status.py#L1-L17",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0d5f4e2eb2b467cb778e5e0b5adcf7cb0b884006938e398e45550ecc752fd92b:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/core/status.py#L22-L41",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2722b266f3233e564c559d8783e660b1dfa015bcc3b4c47f197231bcb12227b3:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_7c269557fb234b4195402090daf191c3",
      "statement": "An error status must never be treated as an absence, because an outage returned as \"nothing found\" renders the service's failure as the caller's fabricated confidence.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/core/status.py#L14-L16",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:df53fafcbe4ca943dccf2ee881fb61199a242e05c4ace0c74a032540ed3643fb:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/errors.py#L64-L72",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:6103b59bcaf8ae4e1024a0aaeb4114e3116f4079541f1e84e326dc9345924a78:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3c3048a13d6c4dc5ba22ef35173ca058",
      "statement": "A per-item check uses its own three-value vocabulary — present, absent, unverifiable — deliberately neither a boolean nor the response status enum, because unverifiable is the answer a boolean would have to lie about.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/core/status.py#L42-L46",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:adacd7c3e73aa4fcdc76b4bc217995d3ed0935d6ac486c57455adb1d4527f1e7:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_b47e43f4bc98411b9e3528d52c1b6156",
      "statement": "Responses come in two families because \"did you find anything\" and \"here is the verdict\" are different questions: Family A search responses can be empty and carry absence machinery, while Family B envelopes report whether the call ran and put the answer in per-item states — so a verify with every claim absent is status ok.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/envelope.py#L1-L22",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:ac51e487a55bbc05176c3c555bd88a121a667e6d32c401ffa99d48103baf3f5d:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/envelope.py#L506-L519",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:f4d879d65a5d56ea16e20bc01bbc94c6f556aa92e35df9282490b0bc89c92348:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_4192c60e652e45478895b3229d6ef1cc",
      "statement": "An empty ok response is made impossible by a model validator that raises rather than asserts, precisely because python -O strips asserts and this check must not be strippable.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/envelope.py#L324-L338",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:792996e214213bd30f2add59dc7ad2677ec88c0b3ab2dedda0504dc7b205fc8a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3318515dcbfd40e4b80a9373db01b744",
      "statement": "A Family A response reports total as the size of the matched set rather than the number of returned hits, with a separate capped flag, so a caller can distinguish a scoping problem from a complete answer.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/envelope.py#L295-L312",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e5ead7f09ca4c35ae21884f4669ad963937bf8ab031fa0e83c534028d3fb0e14:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_84a6410c847c4564affdf403c5c86899",
      "statement": "The weak/needs_scope trust signal is computed server-side from the match-set size against the pages searched, so a client cannot flip it by requesting a larger cap.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/envelope.py#L33-L36",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7361093ea7ae5f88b3be2f0d9485cc899a3d5dd8147f2a269fd3c02c7ebbac3b:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/envelope.py#L62-L69",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:696a063fd051407e9457298bd2e66c072b7a6ba86aa3deb3566887c09fea3cd1:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e7cdbed82ac6417a9088b13f0411e3fa",
      "statement": "Every Family A response echoes back the effective scope, because the service holds no session and the caller's scope is the whole truth about what was searched.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/envelope.py#L313-L322",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:fd5c6508941b3606e2ec30016747a004cb9ece719e4f9872d380feed9cce4a73:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_1200f43b835b4819a7adac2b0305a5d1",
      "statement": "No response carries a similarity magnitude: the previous implementation's fused RRF sum is deliberately not ported, while an ordinal rank remains required — a position is not a confidence.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/envelope.py#L19-L22",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:8bc68ff233109e2627b4d432248da00fa2b5524d03ae8a9361f2215591b5230f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_fd0f2d1091d24331a6031e5cfb6b0392",
      "statement": "One serialiser, wire(), produces the response bytes on every transport, which is what makes an MCP tools/call result byte-identical to the HTTP body for the same request; key order is model field order rather than sorted so status stays first.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/envelope.py#L39-L60",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5cb91e3cb30cd1cfddb2c03441bf851569810b5d70f58a771d3c617c895b61a6:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_7ce4d0159231449ab6b7ee41d6cf3713",
      "statement": "Refusals share one shape whose error code is the contract and whose error and detail fields are always present strings, so a client can branch on the code without a null check.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/errors.py#L55-L63",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:3cd3da48373d1bbda842058c928c85dce88646c0dadd4531c2e814ef0253f000:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_1a487c836fa8444dbfa9a10e7310a2c7",
      "statement": "CODES_BY_STATUS documents every refusal code by HTTP status and is published in the schema's own description, but is deliberately documentation rather than a validated enum because caps.py and the tools own their codes and a second declaration would drift.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/errors.py#L36-L54",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2ed2c587b0f14651c43d64d2e2a0c22c19f5e945965338d21959d5def0f46c79:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_0b04ed9c53f24a7aa0c458540bd364ea",
      "statement": "The refusal model is the only model in the service declared extra=allow, because a refusal's details must name the bound that was hit — limit and requested on a cap, retryable on an outage, available on an unknown tool — and those keys differ per code.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/errors.py#L15-L26",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:ff174559cb8f87bb93542155e1b19f5498f5ee40c71ca9a179ca9f279c31bb77:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/errors.py#L64-L70",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:3840b3cba2a6e183efa616935f9acac51a5f12d1027b2c83d3853148d33959a2:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_9e85fab6262443eeac131928e9c404c6",
      "statement": "Every cap is a typed 400 naming its bound rather than a clamp or a truncation, because a silently trimmed read answers about pages the caller did not ask for and a silently clamped dpi returns an unreadable image — both reporting success.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/caps.py#L1-L11",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:f73826cfb2a032b83f2ddf476206d31eefdd4e8895147156e829b20f18d3a03b:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/caps.py#L39-L56",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:6bb7cad13fc4f855b48644d9990d2ebb3be6ca1f19217978dc68e9bea82759e8:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_10c6a13b7a6049c89d1788f7e9fbaae8",
      "statement": "A read with no pages is refused rather than answered, because it would charge the caller's quota for a question with no pixels behind it — from which any answer could only be the model's prior knowledge; an empty question is refused separately because the question is an input to the read cache key.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/caps.py#L58-L96",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2b735602931925ba2eff2b1c3ba9e8856137380bdcaca02ca03fc82e6bae1fc5:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_f9caae95e56346639dc2b0d798f29231",
      "statement": "dpi is an allow-listed money bound: only six values are accepted, and anything above the pinned answer dpi requires a normalised region because a full page at that resolution is megapixels of raster and tokens of vision input.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/vsir/serve/caps.py#L131-L146",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b370fd934317ac8cee83eaefdb9be759f76b2287c27daaff1e2951106251e75d:"
        },
        {
